# Cursor Rules for Equipment Rental Management System

## Project Overview
This is a .NET 9.0 Web API project implementing an Equipment Rental Management System using clean architecture patterns, JWT authentication, and Entity Framework Core with SQLite.

## Architecture Patterns to Follow

### 1. Repository Pattern
- Always use the generic `IRepository<TEntity>` interface for data access
- Create specific repository classes that inherit from `IRepository<TEntity>`
- Never access `AppDbContext` directly from controllers
- Use the Unit of Work pattern to manage repositories

### 2. Unit of Work Pattern
- Always inject `IUnitOfWork` into controllers
- Use `_unitOfWork.EntityName` to access repositories
- Call `_unitOfWork.Complete()` after making changes
- Never call `SaveChanges()` directly on the context

### 3. Controller Structure
- Always use `[Authorize]` attribute on API controllers
- Use `[Authorize(Roles = "Admin,User")]` for role-based access
- Follow RESTful naming conventions for endpoints
- Return proper HTTP status codes (Ok, NotFound, BadRequest, etc.)
- Use `[FromBody]` for complex parameters
- Validate `ModelState.IsValid` before processing

### 4. Authentication & Authorization
- Use JWT Bearer authentication
- Extract user claims using `_customerService.GetUserNameAndRole(User)`
- Implement role-based access control:
  - Admin: Full access to all operations
  - User: Limited access to own data only
- Always check if user can access the requested resource

### 5. Entity Models
- Use `required` keyword for non-nullable properties
- Include proper data annotations
- Use `DateTime` for timestamps
- Use `bool` for availability flags
- Follow PascalCase naming convention

### 6. Database Context
- Use `AppDbContext` for all database operations
- Configure entities in `OnModelCreating` method
- Use `HasData()` for seeding initial data
- Always use migrations for schema changes

### 7. Service Layer
- Create service classes for business logic
- Inject services into controllers via constructor
- Keep controllers thin, move logic to services
- Use services for user claim extraction and validation

## Code Style Guidelines

### Naming Conventions
- Controllers: `{Entity}Controller` (e.g., `EquipmentController`)
- Repositories: `{Entity}Repository<TEntity>`
- Services: `{Entity}Service`
- Methods: Use descriptive names (e.g., `ReadAllEquipment`, `CreateRentalIssue`)
- Variables: Use camelCase for local variables

### Method Naming
- GET operations: `Read{Entity}` or `Read{Entity}By{Property}`
- POST operations: `Create{Entity}`
- PUT operations: `Update{Entity}`
- DELETE operations: `Delete{Entity}`
- Special operations: Use descriptive names (e.g., `CreateRentalIssue`, `CreateRentalReturn`)

### Error Handling
- Return `NotFound()` when entity doesn't exist
- Return `BadRequest()` for validation errors
- Return `Forbid()` for authorization failures
- Return `Unauthorized()` for authentication failures
- Use descriptive error messages

### Business Rules
- Customers can only have one active rental at a time
- Equipment must be available to be rented
- Users can only access their own data (unless Admin)
- Always update equipment availability when renting/returning
- Use UTC time for timestamps

## File Structure
```
Controllers/     - API controllers with [Authorize] attributes
Models/         - Entity models with proper annotations
Repositories/   - Repository pattern implementation
Services/       - Business logic services
Data/          - Database context and configuration
Views/         - MVC views (minimal implementation)
Migrations/    - Entity Framework migrations
```

## Dependencies
- Always use dependency injection
- Register services in `Program.cs`
- Use constructor injection in controllers
- Keep dependencies minimal and focused

## Security
- Never expose sensitive data in API responses
- Always validate user permissions
- Use proper authorization attributes
- Hash passwords (currently using plain text for demo)
- Validate all input parameters

## Testing Considerations
- Controllers should be easily testable
- Use interfaces for all dependencies
- Mock repositories and services in tests
- Test both success and failure scenarios

## Common Patterns

### Controller Method Template
```csharp
[Authorize(Roles = "Admin,User")]
[HttpGet]
public ActionResult<IEnumerable<Entity>> ReadAllEntities()
{
    // Business logic here
    var entities = _unitOfWork.Entity.GetAll();
    return Ok(entities);
}
```

### Repository Usage
```csharp
// Get all entities
var entities = _unitOfWork.Entity.GetAll();

// Get by ID
var entity = _unitOfWork.Entity.GetById(id);
if (entity is null) return NotFound();

// Add new entity
_unitOfWork.Entity.Add(entity);
_unitOfWork.Complete();
```

### User Authentication
```csharp
var (userName, userRole) = _customerService.GetUserNameAndRole(User);
if (userRole == "User" && !CanAccessResource(userName, resourceId))
    return Forbid();
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid
- Don't access `AppDbContext` directly from controllers
- Don't put business logic in controllers
- Don't skip authorization checks
- Don't use magic strings for roles
- Don't forget to call `Complete()` after changes
- Don't expose internal implementation details
